  "name": "Long-term Measurements of Flow near the Aleutian Islands",
  "description": "In summer 1995, the Alaskan Stream at 173.5W was very intense; the peak geostrophic speed was ~125 cm s, and the computed volume transport above 1000 db, referred to 1000 db, was 9 ᅲ 10 m s. Flow north of the central Aleutians was shallow, convoluted, and weak (2-3 ᅲ 10 m s). A sequence of CTD casts across Amukta Pass, spaced irregularly in time during 1993-1996, showed a mean northward (southward) geostrophic transport of 1.0 (0.4) ᅲ 10 m s, for a net flow into the Bering Sea of 0.6 ᅲ 10 m s. The source of this flow was the Alaskan Stream except in 1995, when it was Bering Sea water. Results from two 13-month current moorings west and east of the pass were quite different. To the west, flow was weak and variable and appeared to have a barotropic component. To the east, flow was stronger and unidirectional eastward. [ Reference: Reed, R.K., and P.J. Stabeno (1997): Long-term measurements of flow near the Aleutian Islands. J. Mar. Res., 55, 565 - 575. ]\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: A report on long-term measurements of flow near the Aleutian Islands",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23563846",
